Subject: Extending or editing a hashed shape in place retires what the address-keyed caches recorded about it

What this does
  - `js_shape_prepare_update`'s in-place branch (a hashed shape with a single referrer is unhashed and then edited: a delete, a flag change, a conversion to an accessor) and
    `add_property`'s in-place append (a hashed single-referrer shape that has no matching transition gets one more property appended) move `rt->shape_free_epoch` before they edit.

Why we need it
  The global stub cache (the megamorphic read cache) records (receiver shape pointer, atom, holder, offset) and validates an entry by the shape pointer and `shape_free_epoch`. An in-place edit
  changes what the shape says without changing its address and without moving the epoch, so the entry stayed valid for a shape it no longer described:
      var P = {p: 'proto'}; var T = Object.create(P); /* a read site made megamorphic and warmed on T */  T.p = 'own';  /* appends in place */   rd(T) -> 'proto'   (spec: 'own')
      delete T.p with a deeper prototype that has p                                                         rd(T) -> undefined (spec: the deeper value)
      delete P.p, T reading through P                                                                       rd(T) -> undefined (spec: the deeper value)
  Reproduced on the interpreter alone (no AOT) with and without patch 0419: .local/kit/cases/stub_append.js, stub_delete.js, stub_protodel.js print `BAD ...` lines; node prints none.
  After this patch all five stub_*.js tests (those three, stub_define.js and stub_addw.js) print no BAD line.
  The same move retires the raw-pointer caches of the compiled code that already compare `shape_free_epoch` (the wrapper cache of 0418).

Soundness
  A hashed shape is edited in place only when its reference count is 1 and the edit is one of the two paths above; every other mutation clones (js_shape_prepare_update with a count above 1, add_property's clone branch)
  or allocates a new shape. In steady state the two paths are rare (0 per unit in raytrace and deltablue; creating the first object of a new shape shape once), so the extra moves cost nothing measurable.
  Unhashed shapes are not cached by address: every fill of a cache that names a shape by address hashes the shape first (`regvm_shape_hashify`).

Functions touched
  `js_shape_prepare_update`, `add_property`.

No.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -14807,0 +14808,4 @@
+    /* A hashed shape that no other object or cache references and for which no transition exists is extended in place: the
+       same address now names a shape with one more property. See js_shape_prepare_update. */
+    if (p->shape->is_hashed)
+        ctx->rt->shape_free_epoch++;
@@ -16816,0 +16821,4 @@
+            /* The shape is about to be edited where it stands, at the same address. Every cache that names a shape by its
+               address (the global stub cache, the compiled code's statics) validates with shape_free_epoch, so this move
+               retires what they recorded about it. */
+            ctx->rt->shape_free_epoch++;
